[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"ACID Properties and Transaction Isolation Levels in Microsoft SQL Server"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data integrity is paramount in relational database management systems. Microsoft SQL Server ensures reliability through ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) and manages concurrency using transaction isolation levels. This tutorial explores these core concepts with practical SQL examples."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ACID Properties"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Atomicity: Ensures that all operations within a work unit succeed or the entire transaction is aborted. SQL Server guarantees this using the BEGIN TRANSACTION, COMMIT, and ROLLBACK commands."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consistency: Ensures a transaction brings the database from one valid state to another, maintaining all predefined rules such as foreign keys, constraints, and triggers."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolation: Determines how transaction integrity is visible to other concurrent users and processes, managed via transaction isolation levels."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Durability: Guarantees that once a transaction has been committed, it will remain permanent even in the event of a system crash, achieved primarily through the Write-Ahead Log (WAL)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Demonstrating Atomicity and Rollback"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following T-SQL script demonstrates atomicity. If any error occurs during the execution, the transaction rolls back, preventing partial updates."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRANSACTION;

BEGIN TRY
    -- Perform first update
    UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 100 WHERE AccountID = 1;

    -- Simulate an error or invalid condition
    -- UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE AccountID = 9999; -- Non-existent

    -- Perform second update
    UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE AccountID = 2;

    COMMIT TRANSACTION;
    PRINT 'Transaction committed successfully.';
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
    PRINT 'Error occurred. Transaction rolled back.';
END CATCH;"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"SQL Server Transaction Isolation Levels"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Concurrency issues like Dirty Reads, Non-Repeatable Reads, and Phantoms can occur when multiple transactions execute simultaneously. SQL Server provides five isolation levels to control these phenomena:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Uncommitted: The lowest isolation level. Allows transactions to read uncommitted data (Dirty Reads)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Committed: The default SQL Server isolation level. Prevents dirty reads by acquiring shared locks, though non-repeatable reads can still occur."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Repeatable Read: Ensures that data read within a transaction cannot be modified by other transactions until it completes, preventing non-repeatable reads."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Snapshot: Uses row versioning in tempdb to allow transactions to read the last committed version of data without acquiring locks, avoiding most blocking issues."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Serializable: The highest isolation level. Completely isolates transactions from one another, preventing phantom reads but significantly reducing concurrency."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Isolation Levels in T-SQL"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can explicitly set the isolation level for your session using the SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL command."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"-- Set isolation level to Repeatable Read
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;

SELECT Balance FROM Accounts WHERE AccountID = 1;

-- Other transactions cannot update AccountID = 1 until this transaction commits

COMMIT TRANSACTION;"}]
